an atom can have the same set of four quantum numbers.</p>\n\n<p>Thus, statement (a) is correct.<br />\n(II) The maximum number of electrons in the shell with principle quantum number, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>Thus, statement (b) is incorrect.<br />\n(III) Electrons in an orbital must have opposite spin, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math> Thus, statement (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>c</mi></math>) is correct.</p>\n\n<p>(IV) According to Aufbau principle, in the ground state of the atoms, the orbitals are filled in order of their increasing energies. Thus, statement (d) is correct.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SA)","_marksId":"614da3f7ebd47351b424f227"},{"id":51423,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Structure of Atom","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Chlorine has two naturally occurring isotopes, ${ }^{35} \\mathrm{Cl}$ and ${ }^{37} \\mathrm{Cl}$. If the atomic mass of $\\mathrm{Cl}$ is $35.45$ the ratio of natural abundance of ${ }^{35} \\mathrm{Cl}$ and ${ }^{37} \\mathrm{Cl}$ is closest to","options":["$3.5: 1$","$3: 1$","$2.5: 1$","$4: 1$"],"answer":1,"solution":"Mav $=\\frac{\\mathrm{M}_{1} \\mathrm{n}_{1}+\\mathrm{M}_{2} \\mathrm{n}_{2}}{\\mathrm{n}_{1}+\\mathrm{n}_{2}}35.45=\\frac{35 \\mathrm{n}_{1}+37 \\mathrm{n}_{2}}{\\mathrm{n}_{1}+\\mathrm{n}_{2}}\\therefore \\mathrm{n}_{1}: \\mathrm{n}_{2}=3: 1$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213424e929d40bb08b1"},{"id":51424,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Structure of Atom","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The allowed set of quantum numbers for an electron in a hydrogen atom is","options":["$\\mathrm{n}=4,1=2, \\mathrm{~m}_{l}=0, \\mathrm{~m}_{\\mathrm{s}}=0$","$\\mathrm{n}=3, l=1, \\mathrm{~m}_{l}=-3, \\mathrm{~m}_{\\mathrm{s}}=-1 / 2$","$\\mathrm{n}=3,1=3, \\mathrm{~m}_{l}=-1, \\mathrm{~m}_{\\mathrm{s}}=1 / 2$","$\\mathrm{n}=2, l=1, \\mathrm{~m}_{l}=-1, \\mathrm{~m}_{\\mathrm{s}}=1 / 2$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\begin{array}{rlrl}\n\\mathrm{n}=2 & \\ell=0 & \\mathrm{~m}=0 & \\mathrm{~m}_{\\mathrm{s}}=\\pm 1 / 2 \\\\ \n& 1 & \\mathrm{~m}=-1,0,+1 & \\mathrm{~m}_{\\mathrm{s}}=\\pm 1 / 2\\end{array}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213b4fc5c2bf0ad1e5e"},{"id":51425,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Structure of Atom","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A certain metal has a work function of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>eV</mi></math>. It is irradiated first with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400</mn><mi>nm</mi></math> light and later with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>800</mn><mi>nm</mi></math> light. Among the following, the correct statement is: [Given:Planck constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mfenced></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>626</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>kgs</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> Speed of light <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mo> </mo><msup><mi>ms</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>]","options":["Both colors of light give rise to same number of photoelectrons.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400</mn><mi>nm</mi></math> light gives rise to less energetic photoelectrons than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>800</mn><mi>nm</mi></math> light.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400</mn><mi>nm</mi></math> light leads to more photoelectrons.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>800</mn><mi>nm</mi></math> light leads to more photoelectrons."],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>Work function of metal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>ϕ</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>eV</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>Energy of photon <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>400</mn><mi>nm</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>hc</mi><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>105</mn><mi>eV</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>Energy of photon <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>800</mn><mi>nm</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>hc</mi><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5525</mn><mi>eV</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>Hence, photon with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>400</mn><mi>nm</mi></math> will emit photoelectrons while photon with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>800</mn><mi>nm</mi></math> will not emit photoelectrons.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SA)","_marksId":"614da4f307f31eb742d33b8a"},{"id":51426,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Structure of Atom","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The maximum number of electrons that can be filled in the shell with the principal quantum number $n=3$ is","options":["18","9","8","2"],"answer":0,"solution":"$3 \\mathrm{~s}, 3 